    "case_id": "587",
    "case_summary": "An 80-year-old Japanese woman with no smoking history was referred to respiratory medicine for dyspnea on exertion. Seven years prior, she was diagnosed with stage IV ovarian serous adenocarcinoma and received TC chemotherapy followed by surgery. After 21 cycles of TC chemotherapy, she started maintenance therapy. She had no notable medical or family history except for ovarian cancer.\n\nNo specific physical findings were observed.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': {'Blood Tests': 'Krebs von den Lungen-6 level: 1250 U/mL (normal <500 U/mL), β-D-glucan level: within normal range, Serum anti-T. asahii antibodies: negative, Autoantibodies related to connective tissue disease: negative', 'Bronchoalveolar Lavage Analysis': {'Recovery rate': '67% (100/150 mL)', 'Total cell count': '615 cells/µL', 'Differential cell count': {'Neutrophils': '1%', 'Lymphocytes': '71%', 'Eosinophils': '12%', 'Macrophages': '16%'}, 'Additional findings': 'Culture, Grocott staining, and cytology were negative'}}, 'Imaging Studies': {'Chest CT Findings': {'First Episode': {'Description': 'Diffuse ground-glass opacity', 'Timing': '15 weeks after initial treatment'}, 'Second Episode': {'Description': 'Diffuse ground-glass opacity, mosaic attenuation, and ill-defined centrilobular nodules', 'Timing': '15 weeks after second treatment'}}}}",
